{"content":{"activity_type":"event_msg/item_completed","completed_at_ms":1794431408384,"item":{"aggregated_output":"# DDG\n\nPick how much storage you want to use. Get an offline collection of practical knowledge, maps, software, AI models, and culture that fits. Add space later and the collection grows without throwing away what you already downloaded.\n\nThe bigger idea is what happens across many users. Everyone gets useful local coverage, but qualified alternatives are deliberately spread across different drives. That preserves more knowledge and different perspectives, and makes the population less dependent on every choice in a single curator's bundle being right.\n\nExisting readers, model runtimes, editors, and agent harnesses make the material usable. DDG would provide the catalog, selection policy, verified acquisition, and maintenance that connect those tools. Users' own work stays outside the managed archive.\n# DDG\n\n## What we want to build\n\nImagine downloading a useful slice of civilization onto a drive. Books, maps, repair manuals, engineering references, source code, software that actually runs, local AI models, music, and whatever else you would want around if the internet disappeared. You pick how much space to give it. The system figures out what fits, downloads it, and leaves you with something you can use offline.\nREADME.md:1:# DDG\nREADME.md:7:Existing readers, model runtimes, editors, and agent harnesses make the material usable. DDG would provide the catalog, selection policy, verified acquisition, and maintenance that connect those tools. Users' own work stays outside the managed archive.\nREADME.md:9:**Read the [full proposal](PROPOSAL.md).** It explains the experience, concrete examples, existing projects, product requirements, candidate algorithms, independently workable components, and the decisions still open.\nPROPOSAL.md:1:# DDG\nPROPOSAL.md:7:That part already exists in various forms. Solomon sells a curated package, Kiwix provides offline readers, and Svalbard builds portable archives from recipes. The interesting part for DDG is how the collection gets chosen, how it improves over time, and what happens when a lot of people use it. The [comparison with existing projects](#existing-projects-and-what-ddg-would-add) goes into what to reuse and where this proposal differs.\nPROPOSAL.md:15:The collection should include the tools to do things with the knowledge. A local model can search the references, help someone understand them, write code, or make an illustration. Existing programs provide the editor, model runtime, terminal, Git, and other tools. DDG puts useful material and working tools on the device and makes them easy to find.\nPROPOSAL.md:27:The **planner** decides what fits on this device. The **scheduler** coordinates selection across devices. Those responsibilities may share code, but distinguishing them makes the behavior easier to explain. The **managed archive** is just the files DDG downloaded and the metadata needed to maintain them. It does not include everything else on the computer.\nPROPOSAL.md:29:Here is the whole flow. Someone chooses a practical-reference profile and gives it some disk space. The publisher has already checked several gardening manuals and determined which provide comparable coverage. The local plan covers the critical baseline, and the scheduler's assignment helps choose which qualified gardening manual this installation gets. Existing readers and a local model can use that manual and the rest of the archive. Later, the user frees more space: DDG keeps the manual and adds engineering references or other useful material. Still later, the user explicitly accepts a newer catalog revision that corrects a bad reference. That update can replace the affected managed file. The user's own code and notes were elsewhere on disk the whole time.\nPROPOSAL.md:41:An app should not be the only entry point. CLI and TUI interfaces need to support provisioning, searching, inspecting the plan, and updating on machines without a desktop. The archive should remain useful through ordinary readers and file tools if DDG itself stops working.\nPROPOSAL.md:66:The baseline needs evidence. Existing survival collections provide a starting comparison for what subjects people expect, how much space those subjects consume, and where their coverage is thin. Evaluate DDG's proposed collections against those profiles, inspect practical examples, and tune the policy. A giant manually labeled ontology of critical facts is not a prerequisite. The definition can evolve as the catalog, ranking evidence, and evaluation cases improve.\nPROPOSAL.md:78:This shared-preservation behavior belongs in ordinary selection. It is not a separate opt-in partition of the drive. Network uploading is a different decision and remains optional. Storing a complete book locally does not imply permission to upload it, and DDG should not silently turn on seeding.\nPROPOSAL.md:92:For example, a user finishes downloading an emergency library and later frees more space. DDG adds an engineering reference, additional maps, or another useful model. The existing emergency books stay put. If the catalog itself is updated later because a book was superseded or a ranking error was corrected, replacing managed files can be part of that separate, explicit update.\nPROPOSAL.md:106:| Candidate source | Useful contribution | What DDG would still need to decide |\nPROPOSAL.md:208:**Product requirement.** Reuse existing readers, inference engines, editors, agent harnesses, and build tools whenever they do the job. DDG's contribution is the catalog, selection, acquisition, and working configuration that connect them.\nPROPOSAL.md:212:DDG does not manage the future work people create with those programs. Their code, notes, documents, and generated images live elsewhere on disk, outside DDG's catalog, storage allocation, indexing, and update logic. DDG may tell an agent how to find the archive; that does not turn the rest of the filesystem into part of the archive. A deliberate later submission to a public catalog is a separate contribution.\nPROPOSAL.md:214:Keep downloaded material as data unless an approved workflow explicitly uses it as code. A book or retrieved page should not acquire authority to change the host merely because it contains instructions. The existing harness's permission and isolation mechanisms should do as much of this work as possible. DDG should not invent a new general agent security system.\nPROPOSAL.md:234:DDG does not need a custom battery-endurance or power-management subsystem. Hardware-kit builders can assess batteries and solar equipment separately. Reused runtimes can handle their normal resource controls.\nPROPOSAL.md:252:## Existing projects, and what DDG would add\nPROPOSAL.md:254:These projects solve real parts of the problem and are candidates for reuse, extension, or comparison. The comparison describes their published approaches. DDG's additions below are proposed requirements, not claims that these projects could never support similar behavior.\nPROPOSAL.md:256:| Project | Existing approach | What the DDG proposal adds or changes |\nPROPOSAL.md:268:| Ark | A mobile-oriented combination of knowledge packs, maps, private tools, and local AI | Desktop priorities and a managed public archive; DDG leaves personal creative work outside its lifecycle |\nPROPOSAL.md:272:The closest acquisition and archive-building references are Svalbard, Offline Emergency Library, and Last Light. Check their current implementations and licenses before deciding whether DDG should extend one of them. [Svalbard](https://github.com/pkronstrom/svalbard), [Offline Emergency Library](https://github.com/elhajahmadj/offline-emergency-library), [Last Light](https://github.com/hamtechautomation/survivalai)\nPROPOSAL.md:312:**Product requirement.** Every growing part of the system needs a scalable workflow. A maintainer should design the structure, contracts, and evaluation standards while agents and ordinary automation handle routine ingestion, metadata extraction, scoped changes, and upkeep. Requiring a person to read every new topic or every line of every agent PR would defeat a major reason to build DDG.\n","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","git status --short; head -7 README.md; head -5 PROPOSAL.md; rg -n 'PROPOSAL|README|DDG' README.md PROPOSAL.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/ddg","duration":{"nanos":10083,"secs":0},"exit_code":0,"id":"exec-7ec32dc2-7140-41d0-90ae-4554e4b2f71c","parsed_cmd":[{"cmd":"git status --short; head -7 README.md; head -5 PROPOSAL.md; rg -n 'PROPOSAL|README|DDG' README.md PROPOSAL.md","type":"unknown"}],"process_id":"58740","source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","type":"CommandExecution"},"started_at_ms":1794431408384,"thread_id":"01a1c82d-7b59-7250-99b4-38e078303482","turn_id":"01a1cc72-4170-76b3-9024-bebeb7b3c462"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/ddg","head":"be20adabb416601580b6c6a51c177a2a21129a26","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/ddg.git"},"id":"ev2-13119d071ac985bbfbe9b18e9dfb2a9b72b078fda7f1e9d55153527d6d1a90b9","kind":"activity","meta":{"actor":{"type":"system"},"model":"gpt-6.1-sol","provider":"openai","session_id":"01a1c82d-7b59-7250-99b4-38e078303482","source":"codex","source_version":"0.167.1"},"occurred_at":"2026-11-11T21:10:08.384Z","producer":{"tool":"cledger","version":"0.27.1"},"raw":{"data":{"ordinal":998,"payload":{"completed_at_ms":1794431408384,"item":{"aggregated_output":"# DDG\n\nPick how much storage you want to use. Get an offline collection of practical knowledge, maps, software, AI models, and culture that fits. Add space later and the collection grows without throwing away what you already downloaded.\n\nThe bigger idea is what happens across many users. Everyone gets useful local coverage, but qualified alternatives are deliberately spread across different drives. That preserves mor