{"content":{"blocks":[{"content":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"5501f7\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000010083,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":2029,\"output\":\"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 2029)\\nTotal output lines: 35\\n\\n# DDG\\n\\nPick how much storage you want to use. Get an offline collection of practical knowledge, maps, software, AI models, and culture that fits. Add space later and the collection grows without throwing away what you already downloaded.\\n\\nThe bigger idea is what happens across many users. Everyone gets useful local coverage, but qualified alternatives are deliberately spread across different drives. That preserves more knowledge and different perspectives, and makes the population less dependent on every choice in a single curator's bundle being right.\\n\\nExisting readers, model runtimes, editors, and agent harnesses make the material usable. DDG would provide the catalog, selection policy, verified acquisition, and maintenance that connect those tools. Users' own work stays outside the managed archive.\\n# DDG\\n\\n## What we want to build\\n\\nImagine downloading a useful slice of civilization onto a drive. Books, maps, repair manuals, engineering references, source code, software that actually runs, local AI models, music, and whatever else you would want around if the internet disappeared. You pick how much space to give it. The system figures out what fits, downloads it, and leaves you with something you can use offline.\\nREADME.md:1:# DDG\\nREADME.md:7:Existing readers, model runtimes, editors, and agent harnesses make the material usable. DDG would provide the catalog, selection policy…1329 tokens truncated…real parts of the problem and are candidates for reuse, extension, or comparison. The comparison describes their published approaches. DDG's additions below are proposed requirements, not claims that these projects could never support similar behavior.\\nPROPOSAL.md:256:| Project | Existing approach | What the DDG proposal adds or changes |\\nPROPOSAL.md:268:| Ark | A mobile-oriented combination of knowledge packs, maps, private tools, and local AI | Desktop priorities and a managed public archive; DDG leaves personal creative work outside its lifecycle |\\nPROPOSAL.md:272:The closest acquisition and archive-building references are Svalbard, Offline Emergency Library, and Last Light. Check their current implementations and licenses before deciding whether DDG should extend one of them. [Svalbard](https://github.com/pkronstrom/svalbard), [Offline Emergency Library](https://github.com/elhajahmadj/offline-emergency-library), [Last Light](https://github.com/hamtechautomation/survivalai)\\nPROPOSAL.md:312:**Product requirement.** Every growing part of the system needs a scalable workflow. A maintainer should design the structure, contracts, and evaluation standards while agents and ordinary automation handle routine ingestion, metadata extraction, scoped changes, and upkeep. Requiring a person to read every new topic or every line of every agent PR would defeat a major reason to build DDG.\\n\"}","type":"input_text"}],"tool_use_id":"call_9f05c6a2626d4263b4c8f8d8a2cc2ef9","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"tool_result"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/ddg","head":"be20adabb416601580b6c6a51c177a2a21129a26","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/ddg.git"},"id":"ev2-b5322c54c009b0c1d8ad57fad07cf6b97ffb61b3e498df26d15c7368be4e9b3e","kind":"conversation_turn","meta":{"actor":{"type":"system"},"model":"gpt-6.1-sol","provider":"openai","session_id":"01a1c82d-7b59-7250-99b4-38e078303482","source":"codex","source_version":"0.167.1"},"occurred_at":"2026-11-11T21:10:08.424Z","producer":{"tool":"cledger","version":"0.27.1"},"raw":{"data":{"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000},"ordinal":1000,"payload":{"call_id":"call_9f05c6a2626d4263b4